#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963} :activity/id #uuid "0e238e7b-3085-5b87-b3c4-0f5c35258e51" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130347 :tutorial/content "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b1a9d148-15f7-5878-8f36-f19ee99c49d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "875dc5aa-e4f4-50f8-a30e-d5ed46d77e32" :step/math-academy-id 20099 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128938 :question/id #uuid "d6c73b7b-4981-5908-b694-be7f0e57b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-5490"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455512}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "59537f37-7060-5544-9b4a-84501e450c50" :step/math-academy-id 20100 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128939 :question/id #uuid "b087e601-c054-563c-b7ee-43e18c74ac51" :question/math-academy-id "e-5491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431011}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "8a926ac2-afd2-5b57-8911-c3b8dac86d39" :step/math-academy-id 20101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128940 :question/id #uuid "c22ab531-3377-5827-93d2-a13854b3308c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392038}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5d1fcdf4-d676-5ff5-bf31-d7e371f43f08" :step/math-academy-id 20102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130403 :tutorial/content "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0f0f6d3-c9e1-52a6-bf51-244bb7751a9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4432 :tutorial/title "Eigenvectors That Correspond to Different Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "3314b023-f15c-5f8b-a4e4-aeea702377a2" :step/math-academy-id 27265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129325}]}
